Title:
Spyndicapped — Spy of your users with Microsoft UI Automation (UIA)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Spyndicapped is a Windows proof-of-concept tool that “spies” on user activity by subscribing to Microsoft UI Automation (UIA) events and querying UIA element properties/patterns. It targets GUI-driven data exposure (typed text, copied/changed values, window focus/creation) without relying on classic keyboard hooks, by leveraging COM-based accessibility interfaces intended for assistive technologies. The tool supports a discovery mode (“find”) to enumerate spy-able windows/processes and a “spy” mode to monitor a specific window name, PID, or the whole desktop, optionally logging to a file. It implements automation event and property-changed handlers, plus app-specific parsers/handlers (examples shown for KeePass looting and messaging/web apps like Telegram, Slack Web, WhatsApp Web). This is useful for red teams, malware researchers, and defenders studying UIA/COM-based keylogging and GUI telemetry abuse. It’s interesting because it demonstrates a less-discussed, high-fidelity user-monitoring technique built on legitimate Windows accessibility plumbing.

Technical Focus:
- Microsoft UI Automation (UIA) COM API usage
- Automation event handling (window/UI events) and property-changed events
- UIA tree traversal, element lookup, and pattern invocation
- Process/window targeting by PID or window title; system-wide monitoring
- Application-specific UIA handlers (KeePass, browsers/web apps)

Use Cases:
- Monitor and log sensitive GUI input (credentials, chat messages, form fields) via UIA
- Proof-of-concept “keylogging” without low-level keyboard hooks
- Targeted looting of password managers (e.g., KeePass) using UIA patterns
- Red-team tradecraft research on living-off-the-land accessibility interfaces
- Blue-team detection engineering for suspicious UIA event subscriptions and COM automation abuse

Keywords:
Microsoft UI Automation, UIA, COM, accessibility, AutomationEventHandler, PropertyChangedEventHandler, UIAutomationClient, UIAutomationCore, event subscription, UIA patterns, tree traversal, window enumeration, PID targeting, keylogging, credential theft, KeePass, Slack Web, WhatsApp Web, Telegram, Windows GUI monitoring